# Known-unfixed jobs that read a signing/App secret from repo level, for
# .github/scripts/check-secret-environments.py.
#
# Format: <path><TAB><job><TAB><expected environment>
#
# This is a SHRINKING list, not an allowlist to add to. Every entry is a bug:
# the job reads the secret from a repository secret, which any workflow that a
# pushed branch can add (`on: pull_request` + `run: … ${{ secrets.X }}`) also
# reads, with no approval — branch protection gates the merge, not that run
# (#1232).
#
# These four cannot be fixed from a coding-agent lane: the installation token
# has no `workflows` permission, so a push touching .github/workflows/** is
# refused ("refusing to allow a GitHub App to create or update workflow"). One
# job-level line per site, pushed by a token that may edit workflows, is the
# fix:
#
#     environment: signing          # ios-release.yml build,
#                                   # mac-release.yml build, mac-dev.yml build
#     environment: dev-dispatch     # dispatch-dev-deploy.yml dispatch
#
# The guard fails on a stale entry, so delete the entry in the same PR that adds
# the line. `scripts/preflight/all.sh` runs it in the required `ci` job, which
# is what makes the list honest and stops a new job from joining it.
#
# Platform side (needs a repository admin; the lane token gets HTTP 403 on the
# environments API). Create both environments with a custom deployment branch
# policy, or the job is refused:
#
#   signing       branch main, tag v*  APPLE_DIST_CERT_BASE64,
#                                      APPLE_DIST_CERT_PASSWORD,
#                                      CERTIFICATE_BASE64, CERTIFICATE_PASSWORD,
#                                      APP_STORE_CONNECT_API_KEY,
#                                      APP_STORE_CONNECT_ISSUER_ID,
#                                      APP_STORE_CONNECT_KEY_ID,
#                                      SPARKLE_PRIVATE_KEY, DEVELOPMENT_TEAM
#   dev-dispatch  branch dev           GH_APP_ID, GH_APP_PRIVATE_KEY
#
# dispatch-dev-deploy.yml triggers on a push to `dev`, so its policy has to name
# `dev`; a `main`-only policy would refuse the dev auto-deploy.
#
# Order matters, because an environment auto-created by the first deployment has
# no branch policy: create the environments and their policies, upload the
# values with `gh secret set <NAME> --env <environment>`, verify that a scratch
# branch declaring the environment is refused, and only then delete the
# repo-level copies (their values are write-only, so each is re-uploaded from
# its source). Deleting a repo-level copy before the environment holds the value
# makes the job authenticate with an empty secret.
#
# When all four entries are gone, delete this file; the guard's default keeps
# working with an empty list.
.github/workflows/dispatch-dev-deploy.yml	dispatch	dev-dispatch
.github/workflows/ios-release.yml	build	signing
.github/workflows/mac-dev.yml	build	signing
.github/workflows/mac-release.yml	build	signing
